# The CI `Format check` gate runs `ruff format --check .` on windows-latest,
# and pyproject.toml pins `[tool.ruff.format] line-ending = "lf"`. Without an
# explicit attribute, a fresh Windows checkout materializes CRLF (git's
# core.autocrlf default on Windows runners), so every Python file fails the
# gate -- a repo-wide failure independent of any milestone's content.
#
# Scoped deliberately to the files ruff formats, and to nothing else. The
# M063/M064/M065 dataset bundle fixtures carry sha256 tamper-detection seals,
# and a blanket `* text=auto eol=lf` rule would silently change their bytes
# and break those seals, so the fixtures are NOT covered by the rules below.
*.py text eol=lf
*.pyi text eol=lf

# MILESTONE-063 exceptional byte-seal reconciliation (owner-authorized).
#
# This fixture is committed as LF (blob 800ecb19, sha256 765601...) and has
# never changed since M063 was implemented. Its tamper-detection seal was
# nevertheless recorded from a Windows working-tree materialization (CRLF,
# sha256 ca98478...), a byte form that has never existed in the object
# database. The seal therefore validated only on a checkout with
# core.autocrlf=true, and failed on every clean LF checkout -- including CI.
#
# `-text` disables EOL conversion in BOTH directions for this path, so every
# platform materializes the committed LF bytes verbatim and the seal is
# reproducible everywhere. Correcting the seal value alone would not achieve
# this: it would merely move the failure from Linux/CI onto Windows.
#
# The fixture's own bytes are unchanged by this rule; only the guarantee that
# a checkout reproduces them exactly is added.
tests/fixtures/m063_robustness_study/synthetic_broad_robustness_dataset_bundle.json -text

# MILESTONE-085 review package.
#
# These are GENERATED documents with `--check` gates over them, and the generators
# write LF explicitly. Pinning the checkout to LF as well means the file on disk is
# the file that was generated, on any platform, so a Windows checkout cannot make a
# byte-comparison gate disagree with a POSIX one -- which is exactly how the first
# CI run of this milestone failed. Scoped to this package: the M063/M064/M065
# dataset fixtures carry sha256 seals and keep their exemption from any EOL rule.
external-review/MILESTONE-085/*.md text eol=lf
external-review/MILESTONE-085/*.json text eol=lf
external-review/MILESTONE-085/*.txt text eol=lf

# V1 personal-paper deployment migration SQL (hash-pinned).
#
# `tools/personal_paper_deploy_migration_f3a7c9e2b164.sql` is committed as LF and
# its sha256 is pinned as a literal constant in tools/personal_paper_deploy.py,
# checked by test_pinned_migration_sql_file_matches_its_pinned_hash. Without this
# rule, a fresh Windows checkout materializes CRLF (core.autocrlf), producing a
# different hash and failing that test on Windows CI -- exactly the same failure
# mode the *.py rule above exists to prevent, just for a non-Python file the
# ruff-format rule doesn't cover.
tools/personal_paper_deploy_migration_f3a7c9e2b164.sql text eol=lf
